<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferdinand Marcos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1989">1989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferdinand Marcos was President of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> from 1965 to 1986. He declared martial law in 1972 and ruled as a dictator, jailing opponents and enriching his family and cronies on a vast scale. The assassination of the opposition leader Benigno Aquino in 1983 and a fraudulent election in 1986 provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/people-power-revolution">People Power Revolution</a>, which drove him into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-marcos.jpg" alt="Ferdinand Marcos" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Malacañang Press · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ferdinand_Marcos%27_official_portrait_during_his_second_term_(1969)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ferdinand Marcos was born in Sarrat, Ilocos Norte, on 11 September 1917. His father, Mariano Marcos, was a lawyer who represented Ilocos Norte in the House of Representatives from 1925 to 1931. Marcos studied law at the University of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, where he was a national rifle champion. In 1935 Julio Nalundasan, who had defeated Mariano Marcos in two elections, was shot dead at his home. Ferdinand and his uncle were convicted of the murder, but the conviction was overturned on appeal. After joining the United States Army in 1941 he fought the Japanese, though researchers later found that most stories about his military service were inaccurate or untrue.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Marcos won his father's former seat in 1949, sat in the House of Representatives until 1959, in the Senate from 1959 to 1965, and was Senate president from 1963 to 1965. He was elected president in 1965 and re-elected in 1969. His early policies centred on massive infrastructure construction, funded by foreign lending. A balance of payments crisis in 1969 and growing Philippine involvement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> then triggered social unrest.</p><p>On 23 September 1972 Marcos declared martial law, extending his rule beyond the two-term constitutional limit. A 1973 plebiscite, overseen by the military, ratified his rule; media outlets and opposition politicians were silenced. He lifted martial law on 17 January 1981 and won a third term. The assassination of Ninoy Aquino in 1983 and an economic collapse damaged his standing. He called snap elections in 1986 against Aquino's widow, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corazon-aquino">Corazon Aquino</a>, and allegations of mass electoral fraud led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/people-power-revolution">People Power Revolution</a> of February 1986.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 25 February 1986 Marcos and his family left Malacañang by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helicopter">helicopter</a> for Clark Air Base and flew on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>. He died there on 28 September 1989. The regime committed documented human rights abuses, especially after 1972, against students, farmers, journalists, priests and nuns and political opponents. Estimates generally include about 1,500 to 4,000 extrajudicial killings or disappearances, and tens of thousands imprisoned or tortured. The Marcos family denies responsibility for these violations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ferdinand Marcos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…overthrew the elected government of Salvador Allende, and Argentina in 1976, and repeatedly in Nigeria, Ghana and many other African states; independence leaders banned rival parties; and personal rulers such as Mobutu Sese Seko in Zaire (1965–1997) and <b>Ferdinand Marcos</b> in the Philippines (1965–1986) held power for decades.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>After President <b>Ferdinand Marcos</b> imposed martial law in 1972, the song was deemed seditious and its public performance was banned, with violators facing arrest and detention.</blockquote><a href="/songs/bayan-ko">Bayan Ko</a></li><li><blockquote>Imelda Marcos, wife of the Philippine president <b>Ferdinand Marcos</b>, sang it with Empress Kōjun on a visit to Japan.</blockquote><a href="/songs/patriotic-march">Patriotic March</a></li><li><blockquote>After days of peaceful mass protest, <b>Ferdinand Marcos</b> fled Manila for Hawaii and Corazon Aquino was sworn in as president.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-25">25 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Former Philippine president <b>Ferdinand Marcos</b> died in Honolulu, three years after the People Power Revolution drove him from power.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-28">28 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ferdinand Marcos</b> imposed martial law from 1972, ruling through cronies until the People Power Revolution of 1986 brought Corazon Aquino to power.</blockquote><a href="/state/philippines">Philippines</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sarrat, in what is now <a href="/countries/philippines">the Philippines</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.16,120.65&amp;year=1963" aria-label="Sarrat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7821 1490 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ferdinand Marcos"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8271 1760h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8271 1760h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.16,120.65&amp;year=1963">What was here in 1963? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
